---
/**
 * The live board.
 *
 * Rendered server-side with the current flares so the page is correct before
 * any JavaScript runs — which also means the island hydrates onto real markup
 * rather than a spinner, and the WebSocket only ever has to carry deltas.
 */
import { env } from 'cloudflare:workers';
import Base from '~/layouts/Base.astro';
import LiveBoard from '~/components/live/LiveBoard';
import { hasRole } from '~/lib/auth/types';
import { getViewerRsvps, isoNow, listActiveFlares } from '~/lib/db/flares';

export const prerender = false;

const user = Astro.locals.user;
const now = isoNow();

const [flares, mine] = await Promise.all([
  listActiveFlares(env.DB, now),
  user ? getViewerRsvps(env.DB, user.id) : Promise.resolve({}),
]);
---

<Base
  title="Live Board"
  description="Raids, gym takedowns, trades and remote invites happening right now around Texarkana. Raise a flare and the community sees it instantly."
>
  <div class="live-band art-band">
    <LiveBoard
      client:load
      initialFlares={flares}
      initialNow={now}
      initialMine={mine}
      canPost={hasRole(user, 'member')}
      signedIn={!!user}
      currentUserId={user?.id ?? null}
      canModerate={hasRole(user, 'ambassador')}
    />
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* --- page artwork -----------------------------------------------------
   * Moltres. Formerly the last survivor of a three-birds-one-per-team set
   * on /about, orphaned once that page dropped the "trainers from all three
   * teams" framing the birds illustrated — it stayed there on layout merit
   * alone, an accident the backlog flagged as needing a real home.
   *
   * A raid boss belongs beside the raid board rather than beside anything
   * about teams: Moltres is Tier 5 in its own rotation, so this page is the
   * one place on the site where the art is *what the page is about* and not
   * borrowed decoration.
   *
   * `.live` (LiveBoard.css) is a 900px column centred in the page, same
   * shape as `.legal` was — a large empty margin on a wide screen. Anchored
   * to the *top*, not the bottom the way /about's was: this board is a
   * WebSocket feed, and its height changes as flares come and go. Anchoring
   * to the container's bottom would make the art jump every time the flare
   * count changed. The head — title, lede, status pill — is the one part of
   * this page that never moves, so the art is positioned against that.
   *
   * Horizontally it is anchored to the *column*, not the band.
   *
   * This was `--art-left: calc(-1 * clamp(30px, 4vw, 90px))`, an offset from
   * the band — and the band is full-width while `.live` is a 900px column
   * centred inside it. So the gap between the art and the text was never a
   * fixed thing: it was whatever margin happened to be left over, and it
   * shrank as the window narrowed. Below roughly 1360px the art's box crossed
   * into the column, and because `.live-empty` is a dashed outline with no
   * background, Moltres did not slide behind the panel — the beak landed on
   * top of "Nothing burning right now". Reported from a real window at ~1100px.
   *
   * Pinning the right edge to the column's left edge instead makes the gap
   * explicit and width-independent: 450px is half the 900px measure, so
   * `50% + 450px` is exactly the column's left edge, and 14px holds it off the
   * dashed border. The art still bleeds off the left of the screen the way it
   * was drawn to — `overflow-x: clip` on `.art-band` crops it without adding
   * scroll width — but it can no longer reach the text at any window size.
   *
   * Width follows the margin rather than the viewport. `24vw` was sized for a
   * band that ran the full width; against the column it is simply too big on a
   * ~1100px window, where the margin is 100px — the bird would be a 264px box
   * showing 86px of itself, i.e. a beak. Sizing the box to the margin instead
   * means what you see is the whole animal, small, and it grows into the space
   * as the window widens. The 110px floor keeps it from going microscopic in
   * the last few pixels before the cutoff, at the cost of a little clipping
   * there; the 360px ceiling is the art's own resolution, past which scaling up
   * only softens it.
   *
   * Note this width can never cause the collision again: the right edge is
   * pinned by `--art-right`, so `--art-w` only decides how far the art extends
   * away from the text, never how close it gets.
   */
  .live-band {
    --art-src: url('/art/moltres.webp');
    --art-right: calc(50% + 450px + 14px);
    --art-top: 90px;
    --art-w: clamp(110px, calc((100vw - 900px) / 2 - 14px), 360px);
  }

  /*
   * Below this, the 900px measure stops being the limit and the column
   * fills the screen, so the margin the art was living in disappears —
   * same trigger as /about's mobile override. But dropping to a faint
   * opacity, which is what /about and the hero do there, only works when
   * what is behind the art is body text: this page is opaque flare cards
   * edge to edge, so a faded Moltres does not sit "behind" one, it just
   * collides with its border, half-hidden and half not. The `onnow-band`
   * mobile rule hit the exact same thing — "0.15 of nothing" — and the fix
   * there was giving the art its own strip. There is no equivalent strip
   * to give it here, so it is simplest and most honest to not render it
   * at all rather than ship a visible glitch against every card.
   *
   * Kept at 1000px. I briefly raised this to 1180px, on the theory that the
   * sliver left at ~1001px was too small to read — but the sliver was a symptom
   * of the width, not the cutoff, and hiding the art is not a fix for a window
   * that Moltres is meant to be visible in. ~1100px is a real window someone
   * uses. Sizing the box to the margin (above) is what actually solved it, and
   * with that in place 1000px is again the honest floor: below it the column
   * fills the screen and there is genuinely nowhere for the art to be.
   */
  @media (max-width: 1000px) {
    .live-band::after {
      content: none;
    }
  }
</style>
